================================================================================
Expose the analytics tunables in app.yaml.template
================================================================================

Context: closing out the Lakeflow graph-analytics plan. Every plan item was
already implemented and committed except one gap in the final "settings and
tests" item: `app.yaml.template` declared only
`ONTOBRICKS_ANALYTICS_JOB_ENABLED`, so a deployed app had no visible knob for
the other analytics settings and silently took whatever the code defaulted to.

Changes:

1. app.yaml.template
   Declared the remaining analytics env vars with literal defaults mirroring
   settings.py: `ONTOBRICKS_ANALYTICS_MAX_TRIPLES`,
   `..._PUSHDOWN_ENABLED`, `..._TOP_N`, `..._JOB_NAME`,
   `..._JOB_PAGERANK_ITERATIONS`, `..._JOB_PIVOTS`. Literals rather than
   `${APP_*}` placeholders because the deploy script uses
   `string.Template.substitute`, so every placeholder must exist in the
   environment or the render fails. `JOB_NAME` is deliberately empty, which
   means "derive `<app>-graph-analytics`" and matches the bundle — a
   hard-coded name would break every renamed deploy.
2. tests/units/settings/test_app_yaml_template.py
   New contract tests. The template now duplicates the analytics defaults, and
   duplication rots silently: a default changed in one place and not the other
   makes a deployed app behave differently from a local one with nothing to
   signal it. These assert the template renders under the same strict
   substitution the deploy script uses, has no duplicated env names, and that
   each analytics value *parses* to the same thing the corresponding `Settings`
   field defaults to (comparing parsed values, not raw strings, so `"true"` vs
   `"True"` is not a false failure). Also pins the job toggle to opt-in, since
   job mode needs a deployed bundle and Spark-readable data.

   Verified the drift test is not vacuous: changing the template's TOP_N to 250
   fails exactly that parametrised case, and only that one.

Deliberate deviation from the plan, for the record: plan todo 2 mentions "with
a Neo4j Cypher override" for the SQL aggregations, but the plan body (B5 and
"What genuinely cannot be saved") specifies that Neo4j is unsupported for
pushdown and stays on the in-app path with the cap. The implementation follows
the body — `supports_pushdown` returns False for any non-SQL dialect — so there
is no Cypher override, and the docs say oversized Neo4j graphs are still
rejected. The per-type rollups are also split across `get_type_edge_stats` +
`get_type_predicate_pairs` composed in `PushdownMetrics._build_type_profiles`
rather than a single `get_type_profiles`, which is the same SQL work under a
different decomposition.

Also note: betweenness and closeness now ship as pivot-sampled estimates
(previous entry), which the plan had recommended against. That was an explicit
later decision by the user; the estimates are labelled as such in the UI and
are exact when the pivot count reaches the node count, which is how they are
tested.

